<body>
<main>
<h1>Euro-Office packages</h1>
<p class="lead">Signed apt and rpm repositories for the Euro-Office Community Edition desktop editors.</p>

<p class="note">Unofficial community build by UnityStack GmbH, not affiliated with the Euro-Office project.
Source and issues: <a href="https://github.com/UnityStack/DesktopEditors">github.com/UnityStack/DesktopEditors</a>.</p>

<p>Installing a <code>.deb</code> or <code>.rpm</code> from the
<a href="https://github.com/UnityStack/DesktopEditors/releases">releases page</a> adds this
repository for you, so later updates come through your package manager. To set it up by hand,
follow the steps below.</p>

<h2>Signing key</h2>
<p>Every repository index is signed with the UnityStack package-signing key. Check its
fingerprint before you trust it:</p>
<table>
  <tr><th>Key</th><td><a href="keys/euro-office.asc"><code>@BASE_URL@/keys/euro-office.asc</code></a></td></tr>
  <tr><th>Fingerprint</th><td><code>@KEY_FPR_SPACED@</code></td></tr>
</table>
<pre><code>curl -fsSL @BASE_URL@/keys/euro-office.asc | gpg --show-keys</code></pre>

<h2>Debian, Ubuntu, Linux Mint (apt)</h2>
<pre><code>sudo curl -fsSL -o /usr/share/keyrings/euro-office.asc @BASE_URL@/keys/euro-office.asc
sudo curl -fsSL -o /etc/apt/sources.list.d/euro-office.sources @BASE_URL@/apt/euro-office.sources
sudo apt update
sudo apt install euro-office-euro-office</code></pre>

<h2>Fedora, RHEL, AlmaLinux, Rocky Linux (dnf)</h2>
<pre><code>sudo rpm --import @BASE_URL@/keys/euro-office.asc
sudo curl -fsSL -o /etc/yum.repos.d/euro-office.repo @BASE_URL@/rpm/euro-office.repo
sudo dnf install euro-office-euro-office</code></pre>

<h2>openSUSE (zypper)</h2>
<pre><code>sudo rpm --import @BASE_URL@/keys/euro-office.asc
sudo zypper addrepo @BASE_URL@/rpm/euro-office.repo
sudo zypper install euro-office-euro-office</code></pre>

<h2>Channels</h2>
<p><code>stable</code> has final releases only. <code>testing</code> also has release candidates
and betas. To follow testing, use
<a href="apt/euro-office-testing.sources"><code>apt/euro-office-testing.sources</code></a> or
<a href="rpm/euro-office-testing.repo"><code>rpm/euro-office-testing.repo</code></a> instead.
A package installed from a release candidate sets up the testing channel.</p>
<table>
  <tr><th>Channel</th><th>Newest version</th></tr>
@CHANNELS_HTML@
</table>

<p class="note">Installed <code>v9.3.6-ce.rc.1</code>? Its version (<code>9.3.6-0~ce.rc.1</code>) sorts
above every later 9.3.6 build, so apt never upgrades it (dnf and zypper only from
<code>9.3.6-0~ce.1</code> on), and it does not add this repository. Add the testing channel as above,
then reinstall once; settings and documents are kept:</p>
<pre><code>sudo apt remove euro-office-euro-office euro-office-euro-office-help
sudo apt install euro-office-euro-office
sudo dnf remove euro-office-euro-office &amp;&amp; sudo dnf install euro-office-euro-office
sudo zypper remove euro-office-euro-office &amp;&amp; sudo zypper install euro-office-euro-office</code></pre>

<h2>Do not add the repository</h2>
<p>Create an empty <code>/etc/default/euro-office</code>, or one containing
<code>repo_add="false"</code>, before you install the package. If you remove the repository
file that the package wrote, it is not written again.</p>

<footer>Updated @UPDATED@. Package files are served from GitHub Releases.</footer>
</main>
</body>
</html>
